   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 633 of the laws of 2006 amending the public health law relating to the home based primary care for the elderly demonstration project, in relation to the effectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S5509",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5509-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2, Chap 633 of 2006",
    "lawSection": "Public Health",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5509\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend chapter 633 of the laws of 2006 amending the public\nhealth law relating to the home based primary care for the elderly\ndemonstration project, in relation to the effectiveness thereof\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to continue access to home based medical\ncare for the elderly living in the community as part of a demonstration\nprogram.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill would extend the demonstration program, as amended by chapter\n373 of the laws of 2010, that allows nursing homes that also provide a\nvariety of community based care to provide home based physician, nursing\npractitioner and physician assistant services to elderly patients.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nSome of New York State's most experienced geriatricians work in nursing\nhomes or for long term care providers that operate a continuum of\nprograms both residential and in the community including nursing homes,\nresidential communities and community based programs for the elderly.\nCurrently, the state is actively encouraging nursing homes to down size\nand further push toward community based care. And, many nursing homes\nhave responded by re-shaping the care delivery system to try and meet\nseniors' needs in their community rather than in the nursing home.\n\nThis legislation extends the authority for nursing homes to operate a\nphysician practice to serve community based seniors in their homes.\nAllowing a limited number of nursing homes to test this service delivery\nmodel will allow the state to judge the impact of borne based medical\ncare on the condition of the elderly and disabled, In many cases, home\nbased services, largely paid for by Medicaid could forestall placement\nof people into more expensive long-term care services, such as nursing\nhomes, where the state ends up paying a substantial part of the cost of\ncare through Medicaid.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nS8087 of 2010: Signed chap. 373\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately, and shall expire on January 1,\n2021\n\n",
